    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "1. First, we calculate the number of students who received an A in both courses. There are 7 students with an A in history and 13 students with an A in math, and 4 of them received an A in both courses.\nWe can use the inclusion-exclusion principle to find the number of students who received an A in either history or math. We need to find the total number of students who received an A.\n\n2. Let $C$ represent history students and $M$ represent math students\n\\begin{align*}\n|M \\cup C| &= |M| + |C| - |M \\cap C| \\\\\n&= 13 + 7 - 4 \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{align*}\n\n3.  There are a total of $30$ students\nWe need to subtract the number of students who received an A in either course from the total number of students:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n30 - 16 &= 14 \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\n4. The number of students who did not receive an A in either course is 14.\n\n\nTherefore, the final answer is $\\boxed{14}$.",
